1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,639
Welcome to this new analysis, today we are tackling a topic that always sparks a lot of discussion,

2
00:00:05,639 --> 00:00:10,560
but we will do it in a slightly different way: zero opinions, only raw data in hand.

3
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:15,120
We will dig into the hard numbers to discover who the people

4
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:20,160
who choose to enter politics really are, and I warn you, what is about to emerge will dismantle

5
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:24,359
many of our most deep-rooted beliefs. Are you ready? Let's begin!

6
00:00:24,359 --> 00:00:29,280
Let's start with a direct question. Are politicians really less capable than the people

7
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:34,320
they represent? There is this widespread myth, I'd say almost a cliché, according to which politics

8
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:39,520
attracts only those who can't do anything else, or the least capable, or that it is an exclusive club,

9
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:43,640
a real playground for a privileged elite totally disconnected from

10
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:48,320
everyday reality. But putting aside bar chatter, what do the facts

11
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:54,640
really tell us? Let's move into the first section: the myth of the politician and the old theory of

12
00:00:54,640 --> 00:01:00,560
adverse selection. Let's move to the second section, and here come the surprising Swedish data,

13
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:04,640
a literally perfect laboratory to test these theories.

14
00:01:04,640 --> 00:01:10,400
Now, to understand a bit more, a team of excellent researchers did an incredible job

15
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:15,560
sifting through Swedish administrative records. We are talking about detailed data on

16
00:01:15,560 --> 00:01:21,599
millions of citizens, extremely precise information on IQ, leadership skills,

17
00:01:21,600 --> 00:01:28,240
and income, carefully collected for decades. And you know what? The results completely overturn

18
00:01:28,240 --> 00:01:32,960
the perspective; analyzing the distribution of scores, the vast majority of the

19
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:37,640
general population obviously sits in the middle. But when you isolate the various

20
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:42,560
categories of politicians, the entire curve shifts clearly towards the highest scores.

21
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:47,960
In other words, they achieve significantly better results than the average citizen,

22
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:53,400
in both cognitive and leadership tests. And there's more. The more senior the role,

23
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:58,280
such as a mayor or a member of Parliament, the more the scores rise. In short,

24
00:01:58,280 --> 00:02:03,080
the famous adverse selection mentioned in old economics textbooks literally vanishes

25
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:08,719
into thin air here. But let's try to make this even more concrete. The data make an amazing comparison:

26
00:02:08,719 --> 00:02:13,719
just think that mayors achieve the exact same cognitive scores as

27
00:02:13,719 --> 00:02:18,280
CEOs of medium-sized private companies. We are talking about managers who

28
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:25,439
run companies with between 25 and 250 employees. Not bad, right? And members of Parliament,

29
00:02:25,439 --> 00:02:29,560
they rank even higher. This is a real final blow to the

30
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:35,120
myth of political incompetence. And we come to the third section: family privilege

31
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:40,419
or merit. What role does the family of origin play? At this point, a very legitimate doubt

32
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:46,020
arises. It is right to ask it. Perhaps these high scores are just the result

33
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:50,820
of a privileged background. Maybe politicians are simply born into rich families

34
00:02:50,820 --> 00:02:55,700
that guarantee them the best schools from day one? To understand this point, we must

35
00:02:55,700 --> 00:03:01,380
distinguish between two worlds. On one hand, elitism, where political power passes to those who come

36
00:03:01,380 --> 00:03:06,060
from wealthy families, making privilege the real key to success. On the other,

37
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:11,500
true meritocracy, where power is won by the most capable people, regardless

38
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:17,539
of the starting point. Which of these two models triumphs when put to the test of data? Get ready

39
00:03:17,539 --> 00:03:22,740
because the answer is truly unexpected. The researchers had a stroke of genius.

40
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:27,920
Instead of comparing politicians with the general population, they compared them directly

41
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:33,240
to their own brothers and sisters. And the result is sensational. Elected politicians clearly outperform

42
00:03:33,240 --> 00:03:38,400
their own brothers and sisters in all tests, both cognitive and leadership.

43
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:43,159
Since family members share the exact same social background and the same basic opportunities,

44
00:03:43,159 --> 00:03:47,800
this proves beyond a shadow of a doubt that what makes the difference are the actual

45
00:03:47,800 --> 00:03:52,600
abilities of the individual. No special boost from the family of origin, just

46
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:59,879
pure merit. Let us then enter the fourth section: the true social origins. Do these leaders

47
00:03:59,879 --> 00:04:05,919
really represent all citizens? Okay, we have established that competence is there

48
00:04:05,919 --> 00:04:11,000
and it is real, but where do these leaders come from? Analyzing the income of their fathers,

49
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:16,279
a truly fascinating dynamic emerges. Imagine drawing a flat line: it means

50
00:04:16,279 --> 00:04:20,719
that all income classes are equally represented in society, from the poorest

51
00:04:20,719 --> 00:04:26,199
to the richest. Well, for the fathers of elected politicians, that line is incredibly flat;

52
00:04:26,199 --> 00:04:30,839
they come in a totally uniform way from every single income level. On the contrary,

53
00:04:30,839 --> 00:04:35,479
if we do the same for doctors and top managers, the curve steepens drastically,

54
00:04:35,479 --> 00:04:40,680
which means they come, for the vast majority, from already very wealthy families.

55
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:45,879
This helps clarify what seems to be a paradox, pay attention to this step. Politicians,

56
00:04:45,879 --> 00:04:50,439
having great abilities and holding very high-level roles, end up earning

57
00:04:50,439 --> 00:04:55,959
well and entering the highest income brackets. This is true, but their roots perfectly reflect

58
00:04:55,959 --> 00:05:00,719
the composition of the general population, the very definition of an extraordinary social

59
00:05:00,719 --> 00:05:06,239
mobility. In politics, it seems, doors really open for everyone regardless

60
00:05:06,239 --> 00:05:11,240
of their parents' bank account. But how do you achieve such a perfect balance

61
00:05:11,240 --> 00:05:16,719
on a national scale? Analyzing the data party by party, everything becomes as clear as day.

62
00:05:16,719 --> 00:05:22,359
The different political sides attract candidates from different social worlds. The fathers of politicians of the

63
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:27,240
Center Party tend to have lower incomes, historically being closely tied to the

64
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:32,319
agricultural world. The Social Democrats draw heavily from the middle class and the working class,

65
00:05:32,319 --> 00:05:38,680
while the Conservatives attract candidates from higher-income families. When you put

66
00:05:38,680 --> 00:05:43,080
all these puzzle pieces together in Parliament, the aggregate creates a real miniature,

67
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:49,000
a highly faithful mirror of the entire society. And we are at the fifth and final section:

68
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:56,680
an inclusive meritocracy. The perfect meeting point between competence and social equality.

69
00:05:56,680 --> 00:06:03,480
All the data we have seen today converge on a powerful concept: inclusive meritocracy.

70
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:08,319
The analysis shows that there is no conflict between having highly competent people in

71
00:06:08,319 --> 00:06:14,399
charge and at the same time maintaining broad social representation. Do you know why? Because

72
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:19,680
there is strong positive selection within every single economic class. Parties manage

73
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:24,160
to identify and recruit brilliant individuals even from the very lowest income brackets.

74
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:29,680
Talent, in short, is absolutely not a monopoly of the rich. It emerges from every social

75
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:35,000
class, shattering the idea that intelligence at the top and popular representation must

76
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,280
necessarily hinder each other. Let's wrap things up and summarize the four major revelations of

77
00:06:40,280 --> 00:06:46,120
this analysis. First, politicians show cognitive and leadership abilities above the

78
00:06:46,120 --> 00:06:52,200
general average. Second, this ability is an entirely individual trait, so much so that

79
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:57,860
they brilliantly outperform their own siblings. Third, their social origins are

80
00:06:57,860 --> 00:07:03,560
incredibly equitable and reflect all income levels without discrimination. And point

81
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:09,420
four, there is a virtually minimal trade-off between competence and representativeness. A

82
00:07:09,420 --> 00:07:14,520
healthy democracy knows how to promote the best talents, coming from all, absolutely

83
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:20,319
all, strata of society. And we reach the end and I want to leave you with a provocation,

84
00:07:20,319 --> 00:07:25,500
a question to reflect on. If the cold data show that a democracy can

85
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:30,259
perfectly select capable leaders who, at the same time, are deeply

86
00:07:30,259 --> 00:07:35,340
representative of the people, why do we continue to believe so blindly in the myth of the incompetent

87
00:07:35,340 --> 00:07:40,380
politician? Perhaps the time has really come to stop listening to the usual clichés

88
00:07:40,380 --> 00:07:46,340
and look with fresh eyes at how democratic leadership really works? Thank you

89
00:07:46,340 --> 00:07:50,100
very much for following me in this analysis, I hope these numbers have offered you

90
00:07:50,100 --> 00:07:55,040
a new and stimulating perspective. Keep being curious and always asking yourself the right

91
00:07:55,040 --> 00:07:56,680
questions. See you very soon!